<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Behavioral Patterns on Chege's Blog</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/low-level-design/object-oriented-design-patterns/behavioral-patterns/</link><description>Recent content in Behavioral Patterns on Chege's Blog</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 19:08:07 -0700</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/low-level-design/object-oriented-design-patterns/behavioral-patterns/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>State Machine</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/low-level-design/object-oriented-design-patterns/behavioral-patterns/state-machine/</link><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/low-level-design/object-oriented-design-patterns/behavioral-patterns/state-machine/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="motivation"&gt;Motivation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use it when an object&amp;rsquo;s behavior changes based on its internal state and you
have complex state transition rules. It&amp;rsquo;s the antidote to scattered conditionals
checking current state. Each state know which state comes next and what actions
are valid. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#HelloInterviewLLDDesignPatterns"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="sample-code"&gt;Sample Code&lt;/h2&gt;
